## Start here and next implementation step

Use the canonical open records and actual main source, plus the current
validator report with its scope. A successful per-file report or host harness
does not close the 31-failed-check working-tree lint checkpoint recorded by A10,
prove a kernel ABI, or replace missing integration.

Prioritize concrete bugs on the current implementation path: callback/buffer
lifetime, integer/address bounds, interrupt/gate order, DMA isolation, power
loss, key/PN reuse and regulatory enable paths. Confirm a proposed defect in
current source before changing it; peer commits may already have fixed it.
Add a regression only if it materially tests behavior or an error boundary.
Source-only completion still does not require a hardware dump or owner logs.

Keep the owner-only acceptance record open while continuous hardening and
authorized notebook testing proceed together after implementation/validation.
Reopen a source record for newly required implementation rather than hiding it
under the final physical task. Keep the highest-priority ready work moving;
do not use endless cosmetic cleanup to avoid a missing native driver path.

## Evidence

- Source commits, reference URLs/version/rights, static review and lint diagnostics.
- Theoretical completeness only; no kext build, device scan, packet logs or hardware claims.
- Add offline host tests only where material behavior/error paths warrant them;
